Memoization tackles "overlapping subproblems" by storing the results of subproblems in a cache (e.g., a dictionary or array) after their first computation. When the same subproblem is encountered again, the cached result is returned directly, avoiding redundant recalculation.
```python
memo = {}
def fib(n):
    if n in memo:
        return memo[n]
    if n <= 1:
        result = n
    else:
        result = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
    memo[n] = result
    return result
```
